#
# clang-tidy configuration for AMA Cryptography (audit Issue 9
# close-out — gate is FAIL-CLOSED).
#
# The checks below are scoped to high-signal categories that are useful
# for cryptographic C code without producing a wall of style noise that
# obscures genuine findings.  The exclusions in `Checks:` below opt out
# of style-only checks that the project's own conventions (CSRC_STANDARDS.md)
# already cover; the project's clang-format config is the source of truth
# for those.
#
# CURRENT POSTURE (2026-05): FAIL-CLOSED.  Every enabled check is
# promoted to error via `WarningsAsErrors: '*'` below; the corresponding
# CI job (`.github/workflows/static-analysis.yml::clang-tidy`) no
# longer carries `continue-on-error: true` or an `exit 0` trailer.  A
# new finding fails the workflow.  Four checks that the audit
# Issue 9 close-out walk surfaced as genuinely incompatible with the
# project's cryptographic-C style or as known false-positive sources
# are explicitly dropped from the `Checks:` list with a one-line
# rationale below, NOT silenced per-site (the user-facing contract is
# "the listed categories are fail-closed" — if a category genuinely
# doesn't belong on the list, drop it explicitly, do not pretend it's
# enforced).
#
# Inline suppression comments are FORBIDDEN in the trees this config
# governs.  INVARIANT-13 words it as "absolutely forbidden ... regardless
# of justification" for `src/c/` and `include/`, which is every file this
# job runs over, and as of 5.0.0 `tools/check_suppression_hygiene.py`
# enforces it: a second pass scans every non-vendored `.c`/`.h` under
# those roots and fails on any suppression marker, with no justification
# escape hatch.  (Until then that pass did not exist — the checker
# collected Python files only, so the forbidden-directory branch was
# unreachable and the tree carried one live suppression while the gate
# printed "all suppressions are properly justified" and exited 0.)
#
# So the answer to a finding is to fix the code, or to drop the category
# from `Checks:` below with a rationale.  The one suppression this
# codebase used to carry — three clang-analyzer uninitialised-read checks
# in the shim of the since-removed vendored Ed25519 backend — was resolved
# the first way (zero-initialising the two locals at declaration), and the
# shim itself is gone with the backend.

Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  concurrency-*,
  misc-misplaced-const,
  misc-redundant-expression,
  misc-static-assert,
  misc-unused-parameters,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-misleading-indentation,
  readability-misplaced-array-index,
  readability-non-const-parameter,
  readability-redundant-control-flow,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-narrowing-conversions,
  -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -bugprone-reserved-identifier,
  -cert-dcl37-c,
  -cert-dcl51-cpp,
  -readability-redundant-declaration,
  -clang-analyzer-deadcode.DeadStores,
  -concurrency-mt-unsafe

# Four checks dropped from `Checks:` above with rationale (audit
# Issue 9 close-out walk — surfaced ~50 false-positive-class findings
# that no inline NOLINT could honestly justify):
#
#   readability-redundant-declaration  — ~26 sites where the same TU
#     re-declares an `extern` function whose declaration is already in
#     the public header included earlier in the file.  The repeat-decl
#     improves locality of intent (callers see the prototype next to
#     the call) and is a recognised project convention (CSRC_STANDARDS).
#     Cleaning these up would require deleting every co-located
#     forward-decl, hurting locality with no behaviour change.
#
#   clang-analyzer-deadcode.DeadStores — was firing on the per-word
#     defensive scrubs (`sX = 0;`) inside `src/c/ama_ed25519.c`'s
#     scalar-reduction.  Those have been REWRITTEN in this commit to
#     `ama_secure_memzero(&sX, sizeof(sX))` so the scrub is now
#     elision-resistant (INVARIANT-6 strengthening).  The check is
#     dropped because the SAME kernel of dead-but-deliberate stores
#     can re-appear in similar lattice-arithmetic reduction code, and
#     converting EVERY arithmetic-only `int64_t = 0` to
#     `ama_secure_memzero` would be both performance-pessimal and
#     algorithmically misleading.
#
#   concurrency-mt-unsafe — fires on `getenv()` calls inside
#     `src/c/dispatch/ama_dispatch.c::dispatch_init_internal()`.  The
#     dispatcher's runtime contract (INVARIANT-15) is that
#     `dispatch_init_internal` runs exactly once per process under
#     `pthread_once` / `InitOnceExecuteOnce`, so the `getenv` calls
#     execute in a single-threaded context by construction.  The
#     check doesn't understand once-init gating and would force a
#     no-op refactor (e.g., a project-side `safe_getenv` wrapper)
#     that adds zero safety.
#
# clang-analyzer-core.UndefinedBinaryOperatorResult is NO LONGER dropped
# here.  It was, tree-wide, which left it dead on the ~40 C sources where
# it works.  It is now enabled globally and reports "garbage value" on
# exactly four reads, in exactly three files: ama_dilithium.c:460 (pointwise
# Montgomery), ama_kyber.c:2635/2697 (basemul, poly_sub) and ama_nistp.c:357
# (limb packing).  Each is an interprocedural false positive with one root:
# the path-sensitive engine explores a call chain in which a fill loop
# (e.g. nistp_select's `for (i = 0; i < nl; i++)`) runs ZERO times — which
# requires a runtime limb count of nl == 0, impossible by construction for
# every shipped curve — and so concludes the output buffer was "returned
# without writing".  The only source "fix" is to zero-initialise a large set
# of hot ECDSA/NTT output buffers purely to satisfy the false path, with no
# safety gain (the ACVP/KAT suites exercise these exhaustively) — the
# performance theatre the project forbids.  So instead of dropping the whole
# category, the CI job (static-analysis.yml::clang-tidy) appends a per-FILE
# `--checks=-clang-analyzer-core.UndefinedBinaryOperatorResult` for exactly
# those three files and runs the check fail-closed on every other source.
# The exclusion is named in one visible place (the CI loop), not per-site
# inline (INVARIANT-13 forbids NOLINT under src/c/, enforced by
# tools/check_suppression_hygiene.py), and tests/test_compiler_warning_gate.py
# pins the excluded set so a fourth file — or a fix that makes one of these
# three pass — fails the gate until the list is corrected.

# WarningsAsErrors flips every enabled check above to an ERROR.  See
# the header for the audit Issue 9 close-out rationale.  PRs that
# touch .clang-tidy must keep this fail-closed posture; a backslide
# to `WarningsAsErrors: ''` reintroduces the very advisory-gate
# pattern this close-out removes.
WarningsAsErrors: '*'

# Header filter:
#   We only check our own headers (src/c/, include/); system headers
#   under /usr/include are excluded — clang-tidy would otherwise drown
#   the output in third-party noise.
#
#   POSITIVE alternation over the real header directories, in POSIX ERE.
#   The previous spelling excluded vendor/ with a lookahead —
#   '(?!vendor/)' — which llvm::Regex does not support: the pattern
#   compiled invalid, Regex::match() answered false for EVERYTHING, no
#   error was printed, and clang-tidy silently suppressed every
#   diagnostic originating in the project's own headers.  Reproduced with
#   clang-tidy 18.1.3: a bugprone-assignment-in-if-condition planted in a
#   src/c header was reported 0 times under the lookahead filter and
#   reported under this one — so the fail-closed `WarningsAsErrors: '*'`
#   gate below covered no header code at all.  Enumerating the permitted
#   subdirectories also means a NEW subdirectory gets a deliberate
#   decision here instead of the default.
HeaderFilterRegex: '^(.*/)?(include/.*\.h|src/c/[^/]+\.h|src/c/(avx2|internal|neon|sve2)/[^/]+\.h)$'

# Format style — match the project's clang-format config.
FormatStyle: file

# Per-check configuration
CheckOptions:
  - key: readability-non-const-parameter.IgnoreMacros
    value: 'true'
  - key: bugprone-suspicious-string-compare.WarnOnImplicitComparison
    value: 'true'
  - key: bugprone-suspicious-string-compare.WarnOnLogicalNotComparison
    value: 'true'

# Style options scoped to the C dialect we target.
User: 'ama-cryptography'
